      '# Error handling and API design',
      '',
      '1. Fail fast and clearly: validate arguments and state at the boundary of a function or module and return a specific error immediately; do not continue with bad data. Error messages say what failed, with which input, and what to do (without leaking secrets).',
      '2. Pick one error strategy per project and follow it (exceptions, Result/Either types, error codes, error values as in Go or Rust). Expected failures (invalid input, not found, conflict) are values or typed errors; programmer errors (broken invariants) are assertions or panics. Never use exceptions for normal control flow.',
      '3. Never swallow errors: no empty catch blocks and no logging-and-ignoring; either handle (recover, retry, fallback) or propagate with added context (wrap with the operation and identifiers, keep the cause). Log once, at the boundary that decides what to do, not at every layer.',
      '4. Clean up reliably: close files, connections and locks in finally / defer / RAII / using; make operations atomic or compensate on failure; keep the state consistent when an error happens halfway.',
      '5. Null and optional values: avoid returning null for collections (return empty); use optional types or explicit "not found" results; make parameters required unless there is a good default; make invalid states unrepresentable with types.',
